his image that's a little bit  annoying though because clearly we're  losing a lot of that capacity to  visualize this normal here why because  the normal vector is a unit vector that  can be between negative one and one for  each component so because it goes down  all the way to negative one  we can't see anything past zero anything  that's below zero just becomes zero so  we're seeing no red at all and no green  at all for like this entire region here  what i would much prefer is having like  the colors being arranged that we can  all visibly see so in other words i'd  like it to be between zero and one  instead of negative one and one because  that way we can at least see everything  so how do we convert this from being  between negative 1 and 1 2 being between  0 and 1 well we can just multiply it by  0.5 and add 0.5 that will obviously take  that negative 1 make it negative 0.5 and  then add 0.5 to push it to 0. and  meanwhile our positive one which is the  other end of the extreme that will be  half to 0.5 and then we'll add back to  0.5 to bring it back up to 1. so no  change there so if we run this code  there we go this looks a lot better to  me because we can actually see the kind  of full spectrum of colors here we can  see that it gets more red as we look to  the right and more green as we look up  the blue mostly stays the same okay so  now that we've done this let's define  some sort of light direction and then we  can use the math to compare the two and  see the angle between them so what  should our light direction be well  looking at this image we know that we're  facing the negative z direction so i  think our light source should kind of be  also facing the negative z direction so  that we can actually kind of see it and  then maybe i want it to come from like  above over here so we want it to be  going in kind of the negative y  direction as well so that it's going  down from above and since my mouse  pointer is around here i want to come  from this side that's also basically  negative x because it's coming in from  positive x into kind of this negative x  space it's going left so what i could do  is simply just define like a directional  vector that's minus 1 minus 1 minus 1  because that's what i'm looking for and  then just normalize it and that'll take  it to be about roughly 0.7 it'll make it  a unit vector and more importantly it  will be in the right direction so let's  go ahead and do that i'm going to write  glm vect3 light direction is going to be  negative 1 negative 1 and negative 1 but  as i mentioned i want to also normalize  it so there we go there's our light  direction so since we determined earlier  that it's the direction of the normal  versus the direction of the light that  we're trying to compare and see how much  it faces the light so the angle that it  shares with the light we really just  need to figure out the angle between  these two but before we do that there's  also one little step that we need to not  forget about and that is the fact that  the light direction that we've defined  is a vector from the light to us  but we're going to be comparing this to  the normal which is a vector of where  this surface faces to so that's kind of  like two different things we have the  incoming directional vector and then we  have an outgoing one i'd like to compare  it to both be incoming or outgoing  outgoing makes more sense so in other  words i'd much rather have a vector from  us to the light instead of from the  light to us and that's really easy to do  i just need to negate the light  direction because that will obviously  just flip it and then once i have that i  can work out like the angle between  these two vectors and i can do that  using a vector dot product because the  cool thing about the dot product is it  will tell us the relationship between  two directional vectors in fact the dot  product of two unit vectors is the same  as the cosine of the angle between them  so what that means if we write this down  in our code is that this kind of dot  product between the normal and the  negative light direction since these are  both unit vectors is going to be the  same as the cosine of the angle between  them and since the cosine function of  course will give us a value between  negative one and one same with the dot  product it will also give us a value  between negative one and one and this  value will determine how much our face  our pixel on our sphere actually faces  the light source so for example what  happens if the dot product gives us zero  what if the result of our double product  is zero well then that would mean that  the cosine of our angle was also zero  and the cosine of 90 degrees is zero  therefore if our dot product returns  zero we know that the relationship  between those two vectors the angle  between them is going to be 90 degrees  that's actually perfect because if  something is facing 90 degrees away from  our light source so if this was facing  well 90 degrees like that you can see  it's it's pretty dark so really it's  just the angles above 90 degrees which  should get brighter and brighter until  it's really facing our light source  perfectly and that is what we're going  to use the dot product for this d here  is actually going to effectively be the  intensity of our color now obviously we  know that if that angle is going to be  greater than 90 degrees the dot product  will give us just like the cosine of an  angle that is greater than 90 will  actually be negative so because of that  what we're going to do is use glm max  the dot product and also zero this is  like a clamp but it's like it's only  clamping one side of it so i'm going to  clamp it between two numbers make sure  it's between two numbers just one so  we're making sure that it's gonna pick  the maximum of these two values since  zero is higher than a negative value if  this dot product is negative then we  will just get zero instead so now what  we can do is we can take our sphere  color we'll go back to our original pink  sphere color and we'll just multiply it  with this dot product and if we run that  code check this out how cool is that we  have our light source perfectly defined  in the place that we defined it at it's  facing the direction we wanted it to be  and we now have some rather beautiful  looking lighting hitting our sphere so  there you go  one small step towards photorealistic  rendering i hope that you guys enjoyed  this episode if you did please don't  forget to hit the like button as an  exercise try and add some controls to  this try and add something like to this  iron gui window that will let you  control the color of the sphere as well  as the direction of the light that kind  of stuff is really fun to play with and  since this is in fact rendering like in  six milliseconds rendering in real time  you'll be able to see that effect in  real time as you play with the values  which is gonna be really cool and will  hopefully help you understand this even  more and i really encourage you to play  with this code for yourself the code  will be uploaded to the github ray  tracing repository which will be linked  in the description below next time  we'll be talking about cameras how they  work and how we can have a camera system  defined inside our application here but  until then thank you guys for watching  don't forget to check out brilliant  using my link in description below and i  will see you guys next time goodbye  [Music]  [Music]  you
 

